Toward a Theory of Universal Cognitive Compute Access

A Market-Compatible Framework for Expanding Access to Advanced AI, Sustaining Innovation, and Internalizing Environmental Costs

Research Draft v0.4 — Final Theoretical Version

Author / Research Coordinator: Ouadi Maakoul

Research Program: For Humanity 4

Status: Theory-building draft — literature-grounded, institutionally calibrated, formally specified, explicitly falsifiable

JEL Classification: O31, O33, O38, H23, Q55, D62

Keywords: cognitive compute capital, AI access, innovation economics, endogenous growth, compute dividend, environmental externalities, market-compatible mechanism design

---

Abstract

The rapid development of advanced artificial intelligence is creating a new economic asymmetry: the productive capabilities of increasingly powerful AI systems are becoming widely relevant, while access to the computational resources required to develop, train, operate, and experiment with such systems remains costly and increasingly concentrated. This paper develops a theoretical framework for studying whether a market-compatible mechanism can expand access to advanced AI computation without eliminating private incentives, distorting competitive markets, or ignoring environmental externalities.

The central hypothesis is that computational access should not be treated solely as a private consumption good. Under identifiable conditions, access to advanced AI can function as a complement to human capital in experimentation, entrepreneurship, scientific discovery, and technological innovation. This framework incorporates three structural innovations: (1) a precise definition of the financing variable τ as a contractual credit pool rather than a generic tax; (2) operationally verifiable criteria distinguishing discovery compute from commercial compute through a Cognitive Compute Ledger; and (3) location-time differentiated environmental accounting internalized directly into the allocation rule.

The framework is calibrated against three existing institutional templates: the U.S. NAIRR Pilot, Canada's Sovereign AI Compute Strategy, and the EU AI Factories program. The proposed contribution is to connect established bodies of economic theory through a new mechanism: commercial AI activity → value creation → compute-access financing → human experimentation → innovation → new economic value → further investment. The paper proposes a strengthened welfare model and a candidate theorem with pre-registered falsification thresholds. The framework explicitly allows the hypothesis to fail. The ultimate objective is not to argue that universal access to frontier AI is always optimal, but to determine whether there exist identifiable economic conditions under which broad access to a minimum level of advanced cognitive compute is a welfare-enhancing, innovation-enhancing, and market-compatible institutional equilibrium.

