1. Introduction

Artificial intelligence is increasingly becoming a general-purpose technology whose effects extend beyond a single industry. Recent research identifies substantial effects of generative AI on productivity, innovation, entrepreneurship, and the organization of work. The OECD's (2024) review of experimental evidence finds that generative AI can increase productivity in several tasks, lower barriers to entry, support creativity, accelerate R&D, and transform business processes, while emphasizing that outcomes depend strongly on the task and the user's experience.

At the same time, access to advanced AI capabilities is becoming increasingly dependent on access to computational resources. This creates an economic question that is not yet adequately represented by conventional models of capital, labor, or digital consumption: What happens to economic growth when the ability of an individual to transform an idea into an innovation depends increasingly on access to scarce computational intelligence?

The question is particularly important because AI has two potentially opposing effects. First, AI can automate existing tasks and substitute capital for labor. Second, AI can enable humans to create new tasks, businesses, scientific hypotheses, products, and technologies. The task-based literature developed by Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018, 2019) provides an important foundation for this distinction. Automation can displace workers from existing tasks, while the creation of new tasks can reinstate labor into productive activity.

This paper asks whether access to AI itself can influence the rate at which humans create those new tasks and innovations. If the answer is yes, computational access becomes more than an issue of consumption or technological inequality. It becomes a potential determinant of the economy's future innovation frontier.

A critical clarification on the nature of compute. Compute is not a public good. It is rival, excludable, and privately provided. This paper does not argue for free compute on public-good grounds. Instead, it argues that compute may function as an innovation-policy instrument — a Pigouvian correction for knowledge spillovers, a liquidity correction for credit-constrained high-human-capital agents, and an access-to-infrastructure policy. This distinction is essential for both economic rigor and political feasibility.

The paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 establishes the foundational economic problem. Section 3 develops the theoretical foundations. Section 4 introduces Cognitive Compute Capital. Section 5 presents the welfare model with environmental internalization. Section 6 formalizes the market-compatible access mechanism, including institutional calibration against NAIRR, Canada, and the EU. Section 7 states the candidate theorem with falsification thresholds. Section 8 develops the empirical strategy. Section 9 addresses limitations. Section 10 concludes.

