3. Theoretical Foundations

3.1 Endogenous Growth and Non-Rival Knowledge

Romer's (1990) theory of endogenous technological change provides one of the strongest foundations for this research program. Romer models technological change as arising from intentional investment decisions by profit-maximizing agents. Crucially, technological knowledge possesses non-rival characteristics and is partially excludable. His framework also shows that human capital and research activity affect long-run growth.

The key implication for our theory is that economic growth is not simply the accumulation of physical capital. It depends on the production of new knowledge. If advanced AI becomes an input into the production of knowledge itself, then access to AI becomes a determinant of long-run growth.

We consider an extension of the Romer production function:

Y_t = F(K_t, H_t, Q_t)

where K_t = conventional physical/financial capital, H_t = human capital, Q_t = effective access to AI cognitive computation. The important question is not whether AI replaces H. The question is whether AI can become partially complementary to H. If:

\frac{\partial^2 Y}{\partial H \partial Q} > 0

then additional AI access increases the productivity of human capital. This creates the possibility of a new policy problem: if human capital is productive but computational access is excessively constrained, society may underutilize existing human capital.

Jones (1995) further shows that the long-run growth rate in Romer-type models depends on the parameters of the innovation production function, not merely on the scale of research effort. This implies that the elasticity of innovation with respect to compute access matters more than the level of compute access. Our theory must therefore be estimated in log-linear or constant-elasticity form.

3.2 Creative Destruction and the Optimal Level of Innovation

Aghion and Howitt's (1992) Schumpeterian growth framework provides a second foundation. Their model treats growth as a process of innovation in which new technologies make existing technologies obsolete. Importantly, innovation produces both positive and negative externalities, including the possibility of excessive innovation through business stealing.

This prevents a simplistic conclusion: "More AI access is always better." Instead, innovation has an optimal level. We decompose the distortion term D(Q) into three components:

D(Q) = D_{\text{admin}}(Q) + D_{\text{alloc}}(Q) + D_{\text{steal}}(Q)

where D_{\text{admin}} is administrative cost, D_{\text{alloc}} is allocative distortion from subsidy, and D_{\text{steal}} is business-stealing externality. The socially optimal access level satisfies:

V'(Q^*) = c'(Q^*) + e'(Q^*, l, t) + D'(Q^*)

The sign of D_{\text{steal}}'(Q) is theoretically ambiguous. Aghion and Howitt (1992) show that business stealing can make growth either too fast or too slow depending on whether the negative business-stealing externality dominates the positive knowledge-spillover externality. If compute lowers entry costs for new innovators, D_{\text{steal}}'(Q_d) > 0 for incumbents, as new entrants capture rents from existing monopolists. The net effect must be signed empirically.

3.3 Automation, Augmentation, and New Tasks

The task-based theory of Acemoglu and Restrepo (2018, 2019, 2020) is arguably one of the most important foundations for the present research. Their framework distinguishes between automation of existing tasks, augmentation of existing tasks, and creation of new tasks. Automation produces a displacement effect, while new tasks generate a reinstatement effect.

This creates a critical distinction for our theory. The question should not be: "Will AI destroy jobs?" Instead: Does the distribution of AI capability alter the economy's capacity to generate new productive tasks?

Let T_A = tasks automated and T_N = new productive tasks created. Then the net task effect is \Delta T = T_N - T_A. Our theory proposes that access to advanced AI may influence T_N. Specifically:

\frac{\partial T_N}{\partial Q_c} > 0

where Q_c is the accessibility of cognitive compute. This is the first testable implication of the theory. Recent OECD work estimates macro TFP effects below 0.66% over 10 years if adoption remains limited, highlighting that Q_c affects both exposure and adoption—a channel that must be modeled explicitly.

3.4 Human Capital and Complementarity

Human-capital theory (Becker, 1964; Mincer, 1974) provides another essential component. Education and skills are productive assets. But if advanced AI becomes a complementary technology for skilled activity, then the productivity of human capital may increasingly depend on access to computational tools.

We distinguish:

H = \text{human cognitive capital}

from

Q = \text{AI cognitive capital}.

The interaction can be represented as:

P = F(H, Q).

The critical empirical question becomes whether:

\frac{\partial^2 F}{\partial H \partial Q} > 0.

If positive, then increasing access to AI can raise the return to existing human capital. This gives the proposed theory a fundamentally liberal rather than socialist interpretation. The objective is not to replace markets. The objective is to allow more human capital to participate in markets.

3.5 R&D Spillovers and Social Returns

The economics of research and development provides another major foundation. The literature reviewed by Hall, Mairesse, and Mohnen (2010) finds that "the private returns to R&D are strongly positive and somewhat higher than those for ordinary capital, while the social returns are even higher, although variable and imprecisely measured in many cases." A companion CEP/LSE paper quantifies marginal social returns between 30% and 50% versus private returns of 7% to 15%.

This provides empirical calibration for the spillover wedge:

\Delta R = R_S - R_P \approx 0.2 \text{ to } 0.35

If this wedge is sufficiently large for compute-supported innovation, the case for subsidized access is strengthened. The key parameter is whether compute-enabled innovations generate larger spillovers than ordinary R&D.

3.6 AI as a General-Purpose Technology

Recent OECD (2024) research explicitly examines whether generative AI should be understood as a general-purpose technology. If AI is genuinely a GPT, then its value is not restricted to the firms that produce AI. Its value emerges through complementary innovation throughout the economy.

Brynjolfsson, Rock, and Syverson (2021) argue that GPTs often exhibit a J-curve: productivity gains may lag implementation due to complementary investments. This implies that the returns to compute access may be delayed and that short-run evaluations may underestimate long-run effects. The empirical strategy must therefore include lagged effects and intangible co-investments (training, organizational redesign).

3.7 Compute as a Measurable Strategic Resource

Sastry et al. (2024) argue that compute is a particularly important intervention point in AI governance because it is "detectable, excludable, and quantifiable, and is produced via an extremely concentrated supply chain." This is important for our framework because compute differs from abstract "intelligence." Compute can be measured in GPU hours, FLOPs, kWh, memory bandwidth, or inference tokens.

This means that an economic theory of cognitive compute can potentially construct an actual accounting system. We can define:

Q_i = \text{effective computational access of agent } i.

Unlike an abstract measure of intelligence, Q_i can potentially be audited. This creates the possibility of a Cognitive Compute Ledger. The concentration of compute supply chains also implies that access is not merely a function of price, but of contractual, regulatory, and infrastructural factors.

