5. The Welfare Model

5.1 Aggregate Welfare with Environmental Internalization

Let aggregate welfare be:

W(Q, l, t) = V(Q) - c(Q) - e(Q, l, t) - D(Q)

where:

· V(Q) = V_{\text{private}}(Q) + V_{\text{spillover}}(Q) + V_{\text{option}}(Q),
· c(Q) = computational resource cost,
· e(Q, l, t) = environmental externality, location and time specific,
· D(Q) = D_{\text{admin}} + D_{\text{alloc}} + D_{\text{steal}}.

The optimal access level satisfies:

\boxed{V'(Q^*) = c'(Q^*) + e'(Q^*, l, t) + D'(Q^*)}

5.2 Decomposition of Value

The value term V(Q) can be decomposed into:

V(Q) = V_{\text{private}}(Q) + V_{\text{spillover}}(Q) + V_{\text{option}}(Q)

where V_{\text{option}} is the option value of future innovations enabled by current experimentation. A single successful innovation can enable an entire tree of subsequent innovations. If compute access increases the number of "seeds" planted, the option value may be substantial even if most seeds fail.

5.3 The Social Planner's Problem

The social planner chooses Q_d (discovery compute) to maximize:

\max_{Q_d} \int_0^N R_i \cdot g(Q_d + Q_i^0, H_i, M_i) \, dF(i) - (p_q + e) \int_0^N Q_d \, dF(i) - K

subject to:

\sum_i e(Q_{d,i}, l, t) \leq \text{Carbon budget}_{\text{discovery}}

The first-order condition is:

\int_0^N R_i \cdot g_Q(Q_d^* + Q_i^0, H_i, M_i) \, dF(i) = (p_q + e) N + K'

This is the precise mathematical statement of the condition that marginal innovation value equals marginal social cost.

5.4 Environmental Accounting: From Appendix to Core Constraint

Compute requires electricity, water, semiconductor infrastructure, data centers, and physical capital. A naive universal-access model could produce a paradox:

\text{AI democratization} \rightarrow \text{compute demand} \uparrow \rightarrow \text{energy/water demand} \uparrow \rightarrow \text{environmental damage} \uparrow

Current environmental data from the IEA (2025) indicate that data centers consumed approximately 415 TWh of electricity in 2024 (1.5% of global demand), with 2025 consumption rising 17% year-over-year to 485 TWh. AI-focused data centers saw an even more significant increase of 50% year-over-year in 2025, and are projected to triple between 2025 and 2030. Total data-center consumption is projected to reach approximately 950 TWh by 2030—nearly double 2024 levels.

Water consumption is also substantial. AI systems alone could have consumed 312.5 to 764.6 billion liters of water in 2025. Carbon emissions from AI systems could have produced 32.6 to 79.7 million tons of CO₂ in 2025—comparable to New York City's carbon footprint.

Critical proposal: The environmental cost function must be spatial and temporal:

e(Q, l, t) = CI_{\text{grid}}(l, t) \cdot p_{\text{carbon}} \cdot \text{Energy}(Q) + WUE_{\text{local}}(l, t) \cdot p_{\text{water}} \cdot \text{Energy}(Q) + \text{Land factor}(l)

where:

· CI_{\text{grid}}(l, t) = hourly carbon intensity of the local grid,
· WUE_{\text{local}}(l, t) = local water usage effectiveness,
· p_{\text{carbon}}, p_{\text{water}} = shadow prices.

We propose the construction of an AI Compute Externality Ledger at project level:

E_{AI} = f(\text{kWh measured}, \text{Water} = WUE \cdot \text{Energy}, \text{CO}_2\text{e} = CI \cdot \text{Energy}, \text{Grid mix hourly}, \text{PUE}, \text{WUE}, \text{Cooling type}, \text{Embodied})

Compute providers should be required to disclose PUE, WUE, and CI per allocation, aligning with the Vector Institute's Data and Impact Accounting schema. This schema provides standardized impact reporting embedded in model cards, covering hardware specifications, GPU-hours, estimated kWh, CO₂ emissions, water use, and model lineage, with low-friction instrumentation via tools such as CodeCarbon, ML CO₂ Impact, and cloud APIs.

Discovery workloads should be routed to low-CI, low-WUE regions or times, with cost shared from the Pigouvian surcharge. A Joule Ceiling should define A_{\min} not in tokens but in carbon budget. We derive this from the carbon budget constraint: if the discovery compute budget is set at 1% of total AI CO₂ emissions (32.6–79.7 Mt in 2025), the discovery budget is approximately 0.3–0.8 Mt CO₂e. Divided across 100,000 recipients, this yields 3–8 kg CO₂e per recipient per year. For a more generous allocation of approximately 500 H100 GPU-hours at a carbon intensity of 400 gCO₂/kWh, the per-recipient carbon cost is approximately 100 kg CO₂e/year—consistent with the proposed Joule Ceiling.

A further consideration is the rebound effect. Subsidizing a scarce good typically increases aggregate consumption of that good (Jevons paradox). If discovery compute is subsidized, total compute demand may rise, potentially offsetting the intended environmental savings from efficient routing. This effect must be modeled explicitly. The net environmental impact of the CCD is:

\Delta E_{\text{net}} = \underbrace{E(Q_d)}_{\text{subsidized discovery compute}} + \underbrace{\Delta E(Q_c)}_{\text{rebound in commercial compute}} - \underbrace{E_{\text{saved}}(l, t)}_{\text{efficiency routing}}

If \Delta E(Q_c) > E_{\text{saved}}, the mechanism may increase total emissions despite internalizing the externality per unit. The carbon budget constraint must therefore be specified in net terms, accounting for rebound.

