7. The Candidate Theorem

7.1 Assumptions

A1. Advanced AI compute is a productive input: g_Q > 0.

A2. Human capital and AI compute are partially complementary: g_{QH} > 0 for H > H_0. The threshold H_0 is an empirical object, not a theoretical assumption. The elasticity of substitution \sigma(H) is threshold-dependent.

A3. Access to compute affects the probability or productivity of innovation: \partial I / \partial Q > 0.

A4. Innovation generates private and/or social returns: R_S \geq R_P > 0, with \Delta R = R_S - R_P \approx 0.2–0.35.

A5. Compute has positive resource and environmental costs: c'(Q) > 0, e'(Q, l, t) > 0.

A6. Commercial AI providers require positive expected returns: \Pi \geq \Pi_{\min}.

A7. A mechanism can allocate a bounded amount of subsidized compute to agents with positive expected marginal innovation value, subject to carbon budget constraints. The mechanism satisfies incentive compatibility (IC) and individual rationality (IR).

A8. The financing mechanism has a marginal cost of public funds \lambda > 0 per unit of public funds. For the contractual credit pool component, \lambda_{\text{contractual}} \approx 0 because it operates via credit accounting rather than distortionary taxation. For public funds, \lambda_{\text{public}} \in [0.2, 0.5], consistent with the Ballard-Fullerton-Ballard literature on the marginal cost of public funds.

7.2 Proposition

Cognitive Compute Access Theorem — Candidate Form

Consider an economy satisfying A1–A8. Then there exists a non-zero subsidized compute allocation Q_d > 0 that increases aggregate welfare relative to laissez-faire if:

\boxed{\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial V}{\partial Q_d} \mid H, M\right] > c'(Q_d) + e'(Q_d, l, t) + D'(Q_d) + \lambda \cdot \tau_{\text{public}}}

and if:

1. The mechanism satisfies incentive compatibility (IC) for effort,
2. The mechanism satisfies individual rationality (IR) for providers: \Pi_{\text{providers}} \geq \Pi_{\min},
3. The allocation satisfies the carbon budget constraint: \sum_i e(Q_{d,i}, l, t) \leq \text{Carbon budget}_{\text{discovery}},
4. The mass of credit-constrained high-H agents exceeds threshold \underline{m}.

The term \lambda \cdot \tau_{\text{public}} is dimensionally consistent with the other marginal terms when \tau_{\text{public}} is measured in units of public funds per unit of Q_d allocated. For the contractual component, \lambda_{\text{contractual}} \approx 0.

7.3 Falsifiable Predictions

1. \gamma > 0 and g_{QH} > 0 in heterogeneous estimation.
2. T_N (new tasks created) increases with Q_c at the extensive margin.
3. Social return ratio R_S/R_P \approx 2\times for compute-enabled innovations (consistent with Hall et al.).
4. Environmental cost per successful innovation declines with location-aware allocation vs. naive allocation.

7.4 Pre-Registered Falsification Thresholds

The theory should be rejected if:

1. \partial V / \partial Q is statistically indistinguishable from zero for marginal users at 5% significance with power 0.8.
2. e'(Q, l, t) > V'(Q) at all observed levels of Q.
3. The allocation mechanism cannot identify productive users better than random (AUC < 0.55).
4. Subsidized compute is captured by already advantaged agents (Gini of Q_d recipients ≥ Gini of wealth distribution).
5. Provider profits fall below \Pi_{\min} for two consecutive quarters.
6. Concentration increases despite subsidized access (HHI increase > 10%).

For the \gamma test, a power calculation indicates that approximately N = 2,000 randomized grants are needed to detect a 10% increase in patenting at 80% power, using the OECD SME survey as a lower bound and the upper-bound effect from the experimental literature as calibration.

