9. Falsification and Limitations

9.1 Alternative Hypothesis: The Theory May Be Wrong

A serious theorem must be vulnerable to rejection. The proposed mechanism should fail if empirical evidence shows that:

\frac{\partial V}{\partial Q}

is small for marginal users, or if e'(Q, l, t) becomes sufficiently large.

9.2 Failure Conditions

9.2.1 Compute saturation. Additional compute produces diminishing or negligible innovation.

9.2.2 Selection failure. The allocation mechanism cannot identify productive users (AUC < 0.55).

9.2.3 Moral hazard. Users consume compute without producing meaningful experimentation.

9.2.4 Capture. Subsidized compute is captured by already wealthy or politically connected actors (Gini of Q_d ≥ Gini of W).

9.2.5 Provider disincentives. The mechanism reduces expected returns enough to decrease private investment in compute infrastructure.

9.2.6 Environmental constraint. The marginal environmental cost becomes larger than the innovation benefit.

9.2.7 Concentration effect. Subsidized access unintentionally strengthens incumbent firms (HHI increase > 10%).

9.3 Limitations of the Current Draft

1. No complete microfoundations for g(Q, H, M) — CES specification proposed but not calibrated.
2. No full general equilibrium analysis — price effects and supply elasticity not modeled.
3. No dynamic optimization — reinvestment loop stability condition not formalized.
4. No mechanism design formalization — IC and IR constraints stated but not proven.
5. No empirical estimates of key parameters — \gamma, \Delta R, and environmental coefficients not yet estimated.
6. No calibration of environmental coefficients — location-specific PUE/WUE/CI data needed.

These limitations define the research agenda.

