11. Conclusion

The emerging literature gives us much of the theoretical foundation required to construct this framework. Romer provides the endogenous-growth logic of knowledge and human capital. Aghion and Howitt provide the dynamics of innovation and creative destruction. Acemoglu and Restrepo provide the task-based distinction between automation and new-task creation. The R&D literature provides evidence that social returns can exceed private returns by a factor of approximately two. Recent AI research provides causal evidence of productivity, innovation, and entrepreneurship effects. Compute-governance research establishes compute as a measurable and strategically important resource. Existing institutions — NAIRR, Canada's Sovereign AI Compute Strategy, and EU AI Factories — demonstrate that shared computational infrastructure is institutionally feasible and already operational.

The remaining scientific task is to connect these bricks. The proposed theory begins with one question:

\boxed{\textit{Can access to cognitive compute become a productive investment in human innovation rather than merely a redistributive transfer?}}

If the answer is yes under identifiable conditions, the result would be a new economic architecture for the AI era. If the answer is no, the same framework should tell us why. That is the purpose of the theorem.

---

Core Equation

The entire research program can currently be compressed into:

\boxed{\frac{\partial V_{\text{innovation}}}{\partial Q} > \frac{\partial c_{\text{compute}}}{\partial Q} + \frac{\partial e_{\text{environment}}}{\partial Q}(l, t) + \frac{\partial D_{\text{allocation}}}{\partial Q} + \lambda \tau_{\text{public}}}

subject to:

\boxed{\Pi_{AI\ providers} \geq \Pi_{\min}}

\boxed{\sum_i e(Q_{d,i}, l, t) \leq \text{Carbon budget}_{\text{discovery}}}

\boxed{Q \geq Q_{\min}}

for the population or class of agents whose computational constraints prevent otherwise productive experimentation.

The research task is to determine whether these conditions can be derived from a rigorous dynamic model, under what assumptions they hold, and whether empirical evidence supports or rejects them. This is not yet the theorem. It is the scaffold from which the theorem can be constructed.


Appendices

Appendix A: Formal Microfoundations of the Innovation Production Function

A.1 The Threshold CES Specification

We specify the innovation production function as a nested constant-elasticity-of-substitution (CES) technology with a threshold in the elasticity of substitution. Let:

g(Q, H, M) = \left[ \alpha (qQ)^{\rho(H)} + (1-\alpha) H^{\rho(H)} \right]^{1/\rho(H)} \cdot \phi(M)

where:

· Q \in \mathbb{R}_+ is effective computational access,
· H \in \mathbb{R}_+ is human capital,
· M \in \mathbb{R}_+^k is a vector of complementary assets,
· q \in \mathbb{R}_+ is model quality,
· \alpha \in (0,1) is the compute share parameter,
· \rho(H) \in (-\infty, 1] \setminus \{0\} is the substitution parameter, which depends on H,
· \phi(M) = \prod_{j=1}^k M_j^{\delta_j} is a Cobb-Douglas aggregator over complementary assets with \delta_j > 0.

The elasticity of substitution between compute and human capital is:

\sigma(H) = \frac{1}{1 - \rho(H)}

The threshold structure is:

\rho(H) = \begin{cases} \rho_0 & \text{if } H < H_0 \\ \rho_1 & \text{if } H \geq H_0 \end{cases}

with \rho_0 > 0 (so \sigma_0 > 1, substitutes) and \rho_1 < 0 (so \sigma_1 < 1, complements). The threshold H_0 is an empirical object.

A.2 Properties of the Threshold CES

Proposition A.1 (Monotonicity). For all H > 0, g_Q > 0 and g_H > 0.

Proof. The CES aggregator is strictly increasing in each argument for \rho < 1. The Cobb-Douglas term \phi(M) > 0 is strictly positive. Therefore g_Q = \phi(M) \cdot \partial / \partial Q [\alpha(qQ)^\rho + (1-\alpha)H^\rho]^{1/\rho} > 0, and similarly for g_H. \square

Proposition A.2 (Concavity). For \rho < 1, g is concave in (Q, H).

Proof. The CES function is concave for \rho \leq 1 (see Arrow-Chenery-Minhas-Solow, 1961). The Cobb-Douglas multiplier \phi(M) is positive and independent of (Q,H), preserving concavity. \square

Proposition A.3 (Cross-Partial). The cross-partial derivative satisfies:

g_{QH} = \phi(M) \cdot \frac{\alpha(1-\alpha)(1-\rho)}{\rho} \cdot \frac{(qQ)^{\rho-1} H^{\rho-1}}{[\alpha(qQ)^\rho + (1-\alpha)H^\rho]^{2 - 1/\rho}}

For \rho < 1 and \rho \neq 0, g_{QH} > 0.

Proof. Direct differentiation of the CES aggregator. The sign is determined by (1-\rho)/\rho. For \rho_1 < 0, (1-\rho_1)/\rho_1 < 0, which appears to imply g_{QH} < 0. However, the denominator term [\cdot]^{2-1/\rho} has exponent 2 - 1/\rho_1. For \rho_1 < 0, 1/\rho_1 < 0, so 2 - 1/\rho_1 > 2 > 0. The numerator (qQ)^{\rho_1-1} H^{\rho_1-1} is positive. The full expression therefore has the sign of (1-\rho_1)/\rho_1, which is negative. This appears to contradict the complementarity assumption.

Correction. The standard CES cross-partial sign is determined by the sign of (1-\rho). For the CES function F(x,y) = [\alpha x^\rho + (1-\alpha)y^\rho]^{1/\rho}, the cross-partial is:

F_{xy} = \frac{\alpha(1-\alpha)(1-\rho)}{\rho} \cdot \frac{x^{\rho-1} y^{\rho-1}}{[\alpha x^\rho + (1-\alpha)y^\rho]^{2 - 1/\rho}}

For \rho < 1 and \rho \neq 0:

· If 0 < \rho < 1: (1-\rho) > 0, so F_{xy} > 0. This is the substitutes case, yet the cross-partial is positive. This is a known property: in CES, inputs are q-complements (cross-partial positive) even when they are p-substitutes (elasticity of substitution > 1).
· If \rho < 0: (1-\rho) > 0 and \rho < 0, so (1-\rho)/\rho < 0, hence F_{xy} < 0. This is the complements case in the elasticity-of-substitution sense (\sigma < 1), yet the cross-partial is negative.

This reveals a critical distinction: the cross-partial g_{QH} > 0 (q-complementarity) is not equivalent to \sigma < 1 (p-complementarity). The assumption g_{QH} > 0 is weaker than \sigma < 1. In fact, for the CES, g_{QH} > 0 if and only if \rho > 0 (i.e., \sigma > 1). The condition g_{QH} > 0 is therefore associated with substitutes, not complements, in the CES framework.

Resolution. The assumption g_{QH} > 0 must be interpreted as q-complementarity (the marginal product of compute increases with human capital), not as \sigma < 1. The threshold CES should be specified with:

\rho(H) = \begin{cases} \rho_0 > 0 & \text{if } H < H_0 \text{ (q-complements, p-substitutes)} \\ \rho_1 \in (0,1) & \text{if } H \geq H_0 \text{ (stronger q-complements)} \end{cases}

with \rho_1 < \rho_0 so that complementarity strengthens at higher H. Both cases yield g_{QH} > 0. The skill-bias is captured by \rho_1 < \rho_0, meaning the degree of q-complementarity increases with H. This reconciles the model with both the leveling evidence (where \rho_0 is small, so gains are broadly shared) and the skill-bias hypothesis (where \rho_1 is larger for high-H agents, so gains concentrate).

A.3 The Innovation Production Function

Let innovation output I_i be produced according to:

I_i = A \cdot g(Q_i, H_i, M_i) \cdot \varepsilon_i

where A > 0 is a productivity shifter and \varepsilon_i is a log-normal idiosyncratic shock with \mathbb{E}[\ln \varepsilon_i] = 0 and \text{Var}[\ln \varepsilon_i] = \sigma_\varepsilon^2.

Taking logs:

\ln I_i = \ln A + \frac{1}{\rho(H_i)} \ln\left[ \alpha (q Q_i)^{\rho(H_i)} + (1-\alpha) H_i^{\rho(H_i)} \right] + \sum_{j=1}^k \delta_j \ln M_{ij} + \ln \varepsilon_i

For estimation, a first-order approximation around \rho = 0 yields the Cobb-Douglas form:

\ln I_i \approx \ln A + \alpha \ln(q Q_i) + (1-\alpha) \ln H_i + \sum_{j=1}^k \delta_j \ln M_{ij} + \ln \varepsilon_i

This motivates the estimating equation:

\ln I_i = \beta_0 + \beta_Q \ln Q_i + \beta_H \ln H_i + \sum_{j=1}^k \beta_j \ln M_{ij} + u_i

where \beta_Q = \alpha, \beta_H = 1-\alpha, and u_i = \ln \varepsilon_i. The theory predicts \beta_Q > 0 and \beta_Q + \beta_H = 1 under constant returns to scale. The complementarity hypothesis predicts that \beta_Q is increasing in H, which can be tested by interacting \ln Q_i with H_i:

\ln I_i = \beta_0 + \beta_Q \ln Q_i + \beta_H \ln H_i + \beta_{QH} (\ln Q_i \times H_i) + \sum_{j=1}^k \beta_j \ln M_{ij} + u_i

The null hypothesis of no skill-bias is \beta_{QH} = 0. The skill-bias hypothesis is \beta_{QH} > 0. The leveling hypothesis is \beta_{QH} < 0.

