Appendix C: Environmental Accounting Ledger

C.1 The AI Compute Externality Ledger

We propose a standardized ledger for reporting the environmental impact of compute allocations. The ledger is constructed at the project level and aggregated to the mechanism level.

Per-project ledger entries:

Field Symbol Unit Source
GPU-hours G hours Provider API
Model FLOP utilization MFU fraction Provider API
Energy consumption E kWh Provider API / CodeCarbon
Power Usage Effectiveness PUE ratio Provider disclosure
Water Usage Effectiveness WUE L/kWh Provider disclosure
Grid carbon intensity CI gCO₂/kWh Electricity Maps / WattTime
Operational CO₂ CO_2^{op} kg E \times CI
Embodied CO₂ CO_2^{em} kg Hardware LCA
Water consumption W L E \times WUE
Land use L m² Data center disclosure

Total operational CO₂:

CO_2^{op} = E \cdot CI(l, t) \cdot (1 + PUE_{\text{overhead}})

Total embodied CO₂ (amortized):

CO_2^{em} = \frac{G}{G_{\text{lifetime}}} \cdot CO_2^{\text{hardware}}

where G_{\text{lifetime}} is the total GPU-hours over the hardware lifetime and CO_2^{\text{hardware}} is the manufacturing carbon footprint.

Total water consumption:

W = E \cdot WUE(l, t)

Net environmental cost:

e(Q, l, t) = p_{\text{carbon}} \cdot (CO_2^{op} + CO_2^{em}) + p_{\text{water}} \cdot W + p_{\text{land}} \cdot L

C.2 The Carbon Budget Constraint

The discovery compute carbon budget is defined as a fraction \kappa of total AI-related CO₂ emissions:

\bar{E} = \kappa \cdot CO_2^{\text{AI,total}}

With CO_2^{\text{AI,total}} \in [32.6, 79.7] Mt in 2025 and \kappa = 0.01:

\bar{E} \in [0.326, 0.797] \text{ Mt CO₂e}

If allocated across N_d = 100,000 recipients:

\bar{e} = \frac{\bar{E}}{N_d} \in [3.26, 7.97] \text{ kg CO₂e per recipient}

For a recipient allocated G = 500 H100 GPU-hours at an average power draw of 700 W:

E = 500 \times 0.7 = 350 \text{ kWh}

At CI = 400 gCO₂/kWh:

CO_2^{op} = 350 \times 0.4 = 140 \text{ kg CO₂e}

This exceeds the per-recipient budget of 3.26–7.97 kg. Therefore, either:

1. The discovery budget fraction \kappa must be larger (e.g., \kappa = 0.1 yields 32.6–79.7 kg per recipient),
2. The allocation per recipient must be smaller,
3. Compute must be routed to lower-CI regions (e.g., CI = 50 gCO₂/kWh yields 17.5 kg).

The Joule Ceiling is defined as the maximum carbon budget per recipient:

A_{\min} = \bar{e} \cdot \frac{1}{CI(l,t) \cdot (1 + PUE)}

For \bar{e} = 100 kg CO₂e, CI = 400 gCO₂/kWh, and PUE = 1.1:

A_{\min} = \frac{100}{0.4 \times 1.1} = 227 \text{ kWh}

At 0.7 kW per H100, this corresponds to approximately 324 GPU-hours.

C.3 The Rebound Effect

Let Q_d be subsidized discovery compute and Q_c be commercial compute. The total compute demand is Q = Q_d + Q_c. The rebound effect is:

\frac{\partial Q_c}{\partial Q_d} = -\frac{\partial Q_c}{\partial p_q} \cdot \frac{\partial p_q}{\partial Q_d}

If the subsidy increases total demand and supply is inelastic in the short run, p_q rises, which may reduce Q_c. The net environmental impact is:

\Delta E_{\text{net}} = e(Q_d, l, t) + e(Q_c^{\text{new}}, l, t) - e(Q_c^{\text{old}}, l, t)

If the rebound effect is strong, \Delta E_{\text{net}} > 0 even if e(Q_d, l, t) is internalized. The carbon budget constraint must therefore be specified in net terms:

\Delta E_{\text{net}} \leq \bar{E}

