Appendix I: Extensions and Future Research

I.1 Dynamic Mechanism Design

The current framework is static. A dynamic extension would allow the planner to learn about agents' types over time and adjust allocations accordingly. This is a dynamic mechanism design problem with adverse selection and moral hazard.

I.2 General Equilibrium with Endogenous Compute Supply

The current framework assumes compute supply is exogenous. A full GE model would endogenize the supply of compute through investment in data centers, chip manufacturing, and energy infrastructure.

I.3 International Coordination

The current framework is national. An international extension would analyze the strategic interaction between countries in setting \tau, carbon budgets, and access rules. This is a global game with externalities across borders.

I.4 Political Economy

The current framework assumes a benevolent social planner. A political economy extension would model the lobbying, coalition formation, and regulatory capture that determine the actual \tau, carbon budget, and allocation rule.

I.5 AI-Generated Innovation

The current framework assumes human agents use compute to innovate. An extension would model AI agents that autonomously generate innovations, raising questions about the returns to human capital and the optimal level of compute access.

