Appendix J: Glossary of Key Concepts

Cognitive Compute Capital (CCC): The economically usable computational capacity through which a human or organization can augment reasoning, experimentation, design, research, production, and innovation.

Cognitive Compute Dividend (CCD): A market-compatible mechanism for expanding access to advanced AI computation through a contractual credit pool, sovereign co-investment, and Pigouvian recycling.

Discovery compute: Compute used for education, scientific experimentation, independent invention, open-source development, early-stage entrepreneurship, and public-interest research.

Commercial compute: Compute used for commercial production, enterprise automation, large-scale inference, proprietary optimization, revenue-generating agents, and industrial workloads.

q-complementarity: The property that the marginal product of one input increases with the level of another input (g_{QH} > 0). This is distinct from p-complementarity (elasticity of substitution < 1).

Joule Ceiling: The maximum carbon budget per recipient of discovery compute, derived from the carbon budget constraint.

Rebound effect: The increase in aggregate compute consumption following a subsidy, which may offset the intended environmental savings.

Marginal cost of public funds (λ): The deadweight loss associated with raising one additional unit of public revenue.

Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC): A measure of the selection model's ability to distinguish high-productivity from low-productivity agents.

Incentive Compatibility (IC): The property that agents truthfully report their types.

Individual Rationality (IR): The property that agents voluntarily participate in the mechanism.

Provider Sustainability Constraint: The condition that AI providers earn at least their minimum required profit (\Pi \geq \Pi_{\min}).

